00:15:14,779 --> 00:15:19,049
to explicit interpretable features rather
than emerging from distributed

232
00:15:19,049 --> 00:15:23,850
training dynamics, limiting effectiveness
when goals arise organically rather

233
00:15:23,850 --> 00:15:28,289
than through deliberate training on
explicit objective descriptions.
Subsection

234
00:15:28,450 --> 00:15:33,809
Activation Manipulation Direct
manipulation of model activations during
inference

235
00:15:33,809 --> 00:15:37,690
can reveal hidden behavioral tendencies by
disrupting the computational

236
00:15:37,690 --> 00:15:42,169
mechanisms that maintain strategic
concealment. Think of this like

237
00:15:42,169 --> 00:15:45,330
giving someone a small amount of alcohol.
Their normal

238
00:15:45,330 --> 00:15:49,690
inhibitions and self control mechanisms
become impaired, potentially causing

239
00:15:49,690 --> 00:15:52,129
them to reveal thoughts they would
normally keep hidden.

240
00:15:53,090 --> 00:15:57,250
Similarly, adding carefully calibrated
noise to model activations during

241
00:15:57,250 --> 00:16:01,289
inference causes models with hidden
misaligned objectives to reveal

242
00:16:01,289 --> 00:16:04,929
their true behavioral tendencies more
readily than genuinely aligned

243
00:16:04,929 --> 00:16:10,450
models. This fuzzing or intoxication
technique achieved 98% accuracy

244
00:16:10,529 --> 00:16:14,700
in distinguishing alignment faking from
genuinely aligned models. At

245
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:19,059
this point, the textbook shows Figure
7.35. It depicts

246
00:16:19,059 --> 00:16:23,340
